INT. WESTBROOK HIGH - RADIO BOOTH - MORNING

A closet-sized booth. Egg-crate foam on the walls, a mixing board, a dusty microphone. A red ON AIR light glows.

ENID PAYNE-ORTEGA, 17, headphones, ink on her fingers, sits at the mixer. MARCELO FIGUEIREDO, 16, her co-host, drums a pencil on the desk and fidgets with a stack of index cards. Through the glass, the empty hallway.

ENID
(into the mic)
Good morning, Westbrook. It's seven forty-five on a rainy Thursday.

MARCELO
(into the mic)
And you're listening to the Morning Grind.

ENID
Today's news: the cafeteria has run out of tater tots.

MARCELO
Riots are expected.

ENID
Also, announcements.

She slides a card across. Marcelo reads it with his eyes, then, to her horror, stops mid-line.

MARCELO
(into the mic)
Uh. The card says the prom committee needs volunteers.

ENID
(low, off-mic)
That's not what the card says.

MARCELO
(off-mic)
It's the card I wrote.

She snatches it. Reads. Her face drops.

ENID
(off-mic)
You rewrote the announcements?

MARCELO
(off-mic)
I wanted it to be good.

PRINCIPAL KRISTENSEN (V.O.)
(through the PA, severe)
Miss Payne-Ortega. Mr. Figueiredo. My office.

Enid hits the cough switch. Marcelo flicks off the mic. The ON AIR light dies.

ENID
Did she hear that?

MARCELO
Everyone heard that.

ENID
Why would you do this?

MARCELO
Because you wouldn't. You've been writing these cards for two years and you never once said anything real.

ENID
That's the job!

MARCELO
It's the job for a person who is scared.

ENID
Don't.

MARCELO
You asked.

ENID
I didn't ask.

MARCELO
You did, with your eyes. Every morning.

Enid rips off her headphones. Throws them. They hit the foam and slide down the wall. Marcelo flinches. Her chest heaves.

ENID
(very quietly)
Read the card again.

MARCELO
It says "Enid, will you go to prom with me."

ENID
In front of the entire school.

MARCELO
It was only the on-air light.

ENID
It was ON.

MARCELO
No. The cough switch was ON. I checked.

She looks at the board. He is right. The red light is dark. She sits down on the edge of the desk, stunned.

ENID (CONT'D)
You're an idiot.

MARCELO
Yes.

She laughs. Covers her face. He waits, card in hand, trembling.

PRINCIPAL KRISTENSEN (V.O.)
Is somebody going to answer me?

Marcelo looks at Enid. She gives the smallest nod. He leans over and presses the intercom.

MARCELO
Yes, ma'am. Yes. We're coming.
